00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:04.590
WILL CURRAN: Yeah.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.048
So the main datasets that we
have in Google Cloud Storage--

00:09:07.048 --> 00:09:10.650
we've got about one and a half
petabytes of satellite data

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.320
from both NASA and ESA--

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.360
the European Space Agency.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.980
And these are images that are
updated on a weekly basis,

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.280
as more satellite
images come in.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.620
And then we also just
completed onboarding

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:31.740
of over 400,000 open art images
from the Metropolitan Museum.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.460
These images are interesting
because they also include

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.070
annotations of that art--

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:41.160
so for example, what
era they came from,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.430
who the artist was,
what the date was.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:47.250
And then we can run
those like a Vision API,

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.180
and output those
annotations into BigQuery,

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.139
so that between Google
Cloud Storage and BigQuery,

00:09:52.139 --> 00:09:54.180
you can do a lot of
interesting analysis on that.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.530
So there's a lot
of cool datasets.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.600
And I'm kind of getting
excited about them

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.910
and I want to use them.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:04.010
What are the conditions?

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:05.920
Can I use them for
whatever I want?

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.450
Is there licenses?

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:08.679
What are the conditions here?

00:10:08.679 --> 00:10:10.720
WILL CURRAN: So the main
criteria for the program

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.350
is that the data be
open for public use.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:17.410
Some of the datasets carry an
additional requirement that

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:21.250
they cannot be used
for commercial use.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:24.550
But that is explicit on
the license associated

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.560
with the dataset
when you access it.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.534
But for the most part, they're
free for use for public,

00:10:29.534 --> 00:10:31.450
commercial, whatever you
want to use them for.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:32.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.770
So now I'm wondering, since
you were talking about the fact

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.285
that you do ETL--

00:10:37.285 --> 00:10:40.360
the extract, transform,
and load process

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:45.340
to transform that raw data
into an actual dataset, what

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.357
other kind of things
that you do to that.

00:10:47.357 --> 00:10:48.940
And what are the
most important things

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:51.430
that someone might be interested
in doing on their own data

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:52.764
before they load it to BigQuery?

00:10:52.764 --> 00:10:54.138
MIKE HAMBERG:
Yeah, there's a lot

00:10:54.138 --> 00:10:56.540
of pieces to that I think
we've learned along the way.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:57.865
One of the most important
things-- something

00:10:57.865 --> 00:10:59.281
that we always
talk about Google--

00:10:59.281 --> 00:11:01.300
is eating your own dog food.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.424
And essentially, what I
mean by that is trying out

00:11:03.424 --> 00:11:04.340
the data for yourself.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.540
Make sure it works for the
end users before releasing it.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:11.140
So what we'll do is, we'll
go in and throw up a visual--

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.255
plot something in Data
Studio, or run a query

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:14.860
and get a result--

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.500
and make sure that the
values in the table

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.950
are going to work for the
analysts, the researchers,

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.870
the scientists,
the companies that

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.175
are going to use this data.

00:11:24.175 --> 00:11:28.570
A very common thing that we
do is to denormalize the data,

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.940
and maybe take a
piece of information

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:33.130
that you would always
want with that table.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.800
For example, we have
international census

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:36.697
information.

00:11:36.697 --> 00:11:38.530
And in the international
census information,

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.029
it just has codes for
each of the countries.

00:11:41.029 --> 00:11:42.820
And so the codes aren't
really that useful.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:44.486
And every single time
you use the table,

00:11:44.486 --> 00:11:47.020
you kind of need to look up,
like, what does code 34 mean?

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:49.019
And so you'd want to know
which country that is.

00:11:49.019 --> 00:11:51.040
So we might join that
data in ahead of time

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.920
when we load the table
and put it in there.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.980
There's a lot of standardization
that also goes into the data.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:00.220
We try to take a smart look
at, what are some things that

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:03.887
would be helpful for developers,
or for anyone that might

00:12:03.887 --> 00:12:04.970
be using it in the future?

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.720
So we would take fields
that maybe the dataset

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.760
has three different fields for--
a day, a month, and an hour.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.860
And we can take those, and
we'll leave them there,

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:17.100
but we'll add a new
field that's a timestamp.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.580
So that when somebody
goes to plot this data,

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:20.579
they don't have to
concatenate all these fields,

00:12:20.579 --> 00:12:26.500
and transform hours in the
military time or regular time.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:30.310
So we do a lot of analysis of
data ahead of time to prep it.

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.627
MIKE MANDEL: Out
of curiosity, how--

00:12:32.627 --> 00:12:34.960
I don't want to use the word
"dirty--" but how messed up

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:36.470
do you find the
data tends to be?

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.845
Is it kind of all
over the place?

00:12:37.845 --> 00:12:40.235
Do you find it's generally
inconsistent or consistent?

00:12:40.235 --> 00:12:41.860
MIKE HAMBERG: It
depends on the source.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.744
We get some very good data
from NOAA and other partners.

00:12:44.744 --> 00:12:46.535
They spend a lot of
time in quality control

00:12:46.535 --> 00:12:47.890
with their data.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.580
Others sources, not
so much, I would say.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:54.130
But the trick is,
with the government--

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.930
especially in the US here--

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.900
they have this mandate
to open up the data,

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:02.720
so they get the data out
there, but often, it's

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:04.570
not in a usable format.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:09.852
It's in a weird file encoding,
or it's in some arcane setup

00:13:09.852 --> 00:13:12.310
that they have on their side,
maybe with their terminology.

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.310
It doesn't make sense for
the majority of users.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.850
So we end up bringing that
in, trying to parse it,

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:19.250
figure out what's in there,
what's useful for people.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.720
And we do a lot of
joining of tables

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.174
and bringing stuff together.

00:13:24.174 --> 00:13:25.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.610
So now I'm wondering,
you're saying

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:30.340
that you adapt that
raw data before you

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:31.870
even put it into BigQuery.

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:36.460
And I'm imagining that that
could be kind of a big thing.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:37.420
There's a lot of data.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:40.780
You need to process it,
parse it, clean it, whatever.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:42.320
How do you actually do this?

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.800
I imagined I would use MapReduce
or maybe Dataflow, something

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:47.639
like that.

00:13:47.639 --> 00:13:49.180
Can you talk a little
bit about this?

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:51.130
MIKE HAMBERG: Yeah, we
have an internal tool

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.260
that we use to actually
pipe the data through.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:56.150
But we definitely use the
techniques like you mentioned,

00:13:56.150 --> 00:14:00.490
often doing MapReduce to
consolidate rows or expand

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:01.600
rows.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.320
We might go from many
columns to many rows,

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.730
if that makes sense,
or vice versa.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:11.890
And we have a connector
that picks up data via FTP,

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:16.590
or HTTPS, or whatever protocol
is used to post the data.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.390
And then we have a
loader for BigQuery.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:23.410
And we do use the BigQuery
API to load that data in.

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:26.410
MIKE MANDEL: I just assumed it
was a big Drive spreadsheet.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:27.620
That's how I just figured.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.370
MIKE HAMBERG: Well,
it's a challenge, too,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.260
because often, this is
100 million rows of data.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.369
So it's massive datasets
that go through.

00:14:35.369 --> 00:14:36.910
And especially, one
of the key things

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:39.160
that we do that's different
than a lot of other places

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:42.790
you might find this data is,
we have a live connection.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.920
So if that data
updates in 30 minutes,

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.590
we are piping another
iteration of that through,

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.140
which means that people
just need to go to BigQuery.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:52.289
They'll have the most fresh
data that's available.

00:14:52.289 --> 00:14:53.830
MIKE MANDEL: So I'm
actually curious.

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:54.550
You say live.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:55.660
What does live mean?

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:58.120
Obviously, in the big data
space, seconds is nice.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:01.060
But are we talking hours, days?

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:02.299
What's that live feed like?

00:15:02.299 --> 00:15:03.590
Obviously, it depends, I guess.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:05.423
MIKE HAMBERG: Yeah, it
depends on the source

00:15:05.423 --> 00:15:07.010
and how often they update.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:12.620
I would say it tends to mean
hourly, or maybe every five

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:13.830
to 30 minutes.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:15.987
But when they're really
big datasets, even

00:15:15.987 --> 00:15:18.320
someone like NOAA, they're
not posting these that often,

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.970
because they're so
huge, for storage.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
next question, I guess,

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.850
is, you were mentioning that
NOAA, for instance, they're

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:29.210
a very good partner and that
they give really good data.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:32.360
But we also have a lot of
other different partners

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.220
that provide us with data.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.600
Why do they do it?

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.959
Why would they do
this thing for free?

00:15:37.959 --> 00:15:39.500
WILL CURRAN: Well,
in a lot of cases,

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.350
as Mike mentioned
before, especially

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:45.200
with government data,
there's a mandate

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.060
to make this data available.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:49.580
But what we're
learning, especially

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.170
through the
relationship with NOAA,

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:56.480
is not only are they incurring
more storage costs over time.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.919
If they didn't add any
more data, over time,

00:15:58.919 --> 00:15:59.960
those costs are mounting.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.840
But also, they're collecting
more and more data.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:06.560
You know, we're moving into
this Internet of things world,

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:09.870
where there's more
monitoring devices.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:13.670
And so their data is growing
daily, exponentially.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.380
So the problem
they have is, they

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:17.260
have to distribute
this data to--

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.100
they have thousands
of users that

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:21.170
are interested in
this weather data.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:25.190
And so nevermind their
own storage costs,

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.560
the egress charges are
getting out of control.

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:31.760
And so what they see is
valuable from different platform

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:35.420
partners that have these open
datasets programs like ours

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:40.070
is that they can push
distribution to, like, five

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:44.000
platforms, and then point
users to those platforms,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.910
so that they aren't
incurring egress

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:50.150
on NOAA's servers every minute.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:52.190
So we're seeing
this example as we

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:55.642
talk to more and more partners
that have open datasets.

00:16:55.642 --> 00:16:56.600
MIKE MANDEL: Excellent.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.600
WILL CURRAN: And then
there are some other cases

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:01.690
where a non-government
agency that has open data,

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.950
they're releasing that
open data with the hopes

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:08.960
that people will run
like an ML use case on it

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:12.290
to do some interesting things
that they just don't have

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:14.079
the resources to do internally.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:16.609
MIKE MANDEL: So that segues
into my next question, which

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:18.110
is, what sort of
stuff have you seen

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:20.484
people do with these datasets
that have been particularly

00:17:20.484 --> 00:17:21.290
interesting?

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.750
WILL CURRAN: Yeah, so
our own Felipe Hoffa

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.173
had kind of teed this off.

00:17:25.173 --> 00:17:26.839
I don't know if you
remember the Silicon

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:30.890
Valley show where they had
the tabs versus spaces debate.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:35.150
And so Philippe took
the Github dataset

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.550
that we had to kind
of prove this debate.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:41.930
And he ran several queries
to show that, in fact, spaces

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:46.790
were most popular in every
language except ironically, Go.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:48.800
So that was a pretty
interesting analysis

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:52.250
that got picked up by a lot
of the different communities.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.174
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I saw that analysis,

00:17:54.174 --> 00:17:55.090
and I really liked it.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.655
Especially, I mean,
I'm a "Go-pher."

00:17:56.655 --> 00:17:57.655
I write Go all the time.

00:17:57.655 --> 00:17:58.560
MIKE MANDEL: Really,
I didn't know that.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:17:59.384
I had no idea.

00:17:59.384 --> 00:18:00.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Shocking, I know.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:03.170
But the funny thing
is that, yeah, there's

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:05.180
a lot of people that
was like, oh, in Go,

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:07.280
everyone writes tabs.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:08.900
And then in other
language, everybody

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:10.730
writes spaces, which is fine.

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:13.838
But then some languages were
like, well, it's half-half.

00:18:13.838 --> 00:18:15.296
WILL CURRAN: Yeah,
that's not good.

00:18:15.296 --> 00:18:16.046
MIKE MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:16.046 --> 00:18:17.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is not good.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:18.530
Cool.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.570
Is there any other use case?

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.120
I'm thinking about,
for instance, there

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.150
was this really cool contests
with Kaggle, the YouTube thing.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:28.650
MIKE MANDEL: Oh,
the eight million--

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:30.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
the eight million--

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:33.830
do you have any big dataset that
people could use for machine

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:36.909
learning that
cannot be big scale?

00:18:36.909 --> 00:18:38.700
WILL CURRAN: Yeah, like
I mentioned before,

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.970
we're releasing the Metropolitan
Museum open art images .

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:47.600
And then we're also working with
some different third parties

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.090
that provide medical
image datasets.

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:52.190
So these would be
radiology images.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:53.660
And one of the
use cases there is

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:57.680
that the community can
run machine learning

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:01.490
on these radiology images to
try to classify and detect

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:03.350
different anomalies
in those images,

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:06.320
whether they're tumors
or different growths.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:08.600
So we're trying
to find all sorts

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.990
of different large datasets
that are public domain

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:15.080
to provide to the
community to start

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:17.120
building different
classification models.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:17.870
MIKE MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.850
Mike, did you have any other
examples you wanted to add?

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:20.060
MIKE HAMBERG: Yeah.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:22.100
We've been doing a lot
of work around city data.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.470
That's been a hot topic around.

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:26.544
Data.gov and these
cities are opening up

00:19:26.544 --> 00:19:28.710
a lot of their information
about how the city works,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:32.660
and things like 311 calls, or
fire station response, police

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.770
incidents, stuff like that.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:39.260
So [? Raido, ?] who is a
developer advocate on our team,

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:41.760
has been doing some
interesting analysis--

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:43.760
things like, where is
your car most likely to be

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:45.230
broken into in San Francisco?

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:48.547
Or how long does it take the
fire station to get to a fire?

00:19:48.547 --> 00:19:50.630
He did a really cool thing
in New York City about,

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:52.490
which borough is
the rat capital,

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:56.030
based on 311 calls
about rat complaints?

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:58.159
But there's all kinds
of interesting city data

00:19:58.159 --> 00:19:59.450
that's out there and available.

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:02.000
And we're really just
starting to pick up on that,

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:06.560
bring that into BigQuery so
people can dream up new things,

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.170
and also, analyze across cities.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:11.970
I think that's one of the really
big values of the program,

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.820
is taking one dataset
and merging it

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:15.780
with another dataset.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.300
For example, some
of the medical data,

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.190
they merged that
with the census data.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:22.500
And so because we have all
those datasets available,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.440
it's easy to join them, and
pull in other information,

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.310
and make your dataset
more compelling.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:28.140
WILL CURRAN: Yeah,
[? Raido's ?] stuff

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.510
is really interesting,
too, as Mike mentioned.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.960
Because he's also pulling
in weather-related data

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.530
to show how there's different
correlations between events

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:38.590
from the city data.

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:40.840
MIKE MANDEL: Interesting--
so seeing if rain affects--

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:42.548
I don't know-- cab
traffic, or something.

00:20:42.548 --> 00:20:45.620
WILL CURRAN: Yeah, cab
traffic, bike usage.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.876
And he also uses just to show
that you may see correlations,

00:20:48.876 --> 00:20:50.250
but that doesn't
necessarily mean

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:53.097
there's a statistical reason.

00:20:53.097 --> 00:20:54.930
One was-- I can't
remember, Mike-- wasn't it

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.030
something like, on
snow days, there

00:20:57.030 --> 00:20:59.760
was a higher percentage
of last manhole covers,

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.281
or something weird like that?

00:21:01.281 --> 00:21:02.406
MIKE HAMBERG: That's great.

00:21:02.406 --> 00:21:04.020
Yeah, that's something
we should do,

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.822
is the whole spurious
correlation data.

00:21:06.822 --> 00:21:09.280
MIKE MANDEL: I'm also wondering
if there's opportunity here

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.315
to do competitions
between cities.

00:21:11.315 --> 00:21:13.440
Because you know how cities
love to have rivalries.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:14.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh,
yeah it could be fun.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:16.434
MIKE MANDEL: That
could be lots of fun.

00:21:16.434 --> 00:21:18.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm
wondering-- a long time ago,

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:21.810
when I used the NOAA dataset,
one of the things that I ended

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:24.870
up using at the same time
was this other dataset that

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:28.860
allowed me to basically go
from country to continent,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.030
for instance.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:31.860
Is there such a dataset.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:34.615
It's basically like the
Knowledge Graph for Google.

00:21:34.615 --> 00:21:36.615
I don't know if you know
Google Knowledge Graph.

00:21:36.615 --> 00:21:39.750
It allows you to
basically ask about facts.

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:43.620
Is there such a thing that you
can mix with BigQuery easily

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:44.910
to ask that kind of questions?

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:47.050
MIKE HAMBERG: Facts about
countries around the world?

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:48.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, for instance,

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:50.380
let's say that you
have a dataset.

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:53.580
And the dataset is telling you
about things about countries.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.970
But your question is actually,
I don't care about the country,

00:21:56.970 --> 00:21:58.995
I care about the
continent level.

00:21:58.995 --> 00:22:01.664
So now, you want to ask,
well, given the country,

00:22:01.664 --> 00:22:02.580
give me the continent.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.570
Is there such a
generic or general data

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:06.570
set that people can use?

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:08.528
MIKE HAMBERG: Yeah, we've
been building these--

00:22:08.528 --> 00:22:10.350
what we call utility datasets.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.570
And so a pretty good
example of that is,

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:14.880
you might have a data with
addresses or zip codes

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:16.260
that you want to know--

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:19.710
actually, plot these, with
latitude and longitude.

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:22.230
So we've been building these
join tables, essentially,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:22.920
that are there.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:24.930
I don't think we have one
that is country to continent.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.179
But that'd be pretty easy
to set up, since there's not

00:22:27.179 --> 00:22:28.140
very many continents.

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:31.440
But we do have,
especially in the US, all

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:32.900
the different
geographical points

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:35.160
like zip code, state, county--

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:37.530
all that stuff, you can pull in.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:41.310
And then we're doing some stuff
on some future datasets that

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:43.260
will have kind of a
more comprehensive look

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:45.320
at the different data
you can pull together.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:47.195
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Could
you share a little bit

00:22:47.195 --> 00:22:48.932
on what data sets
are you working on,

00:22:48.932 --> 00:22:49.890
or something like that?

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:51.810
What datasets do you
think people might get

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:53.080
excited about that are coming?

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:54.330
WILL CURRAN: Yeah, I'll start.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.610
And then, Mike, you
can add to this.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.560
We're working on, like
Mike mentioned earlier,

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.260
trying to get more
city-themed datasets in--

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:04.200
so like, Austin, London.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.450
I've also started doing
some research on what

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.580
it would take to get Tokyo in.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:13.800
The problem is that in order to
do interesting joint analysis

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:18.270
between these datasets, you
have to have common joint keys.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:21.090
And there's no really
standard schema

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:23.490
for all this municipal data.

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.400
So trying to patch it together--

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:27.630
and it's a lot of the
work that Mike's doing--

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:30.800
and trying to make it so
that it can join together

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:31.740
is quite a challenge.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.590
But I'm hoping that we see more
and more of these datasets come

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:36.900
out of the
municipalities, so that we

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:40.080
can start doing different
analysis across cities.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:43.022
MIKE HAMBERG: And a dataset that
I'm excited about, that we just

00:23:43.022 --> 00:23:44.730
launched, and that
we're also working on,

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:46.390
is around air quality.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:50.266
So we're all wandering around
cities in different places,

00:23:50.266 --> 00:23:52.140
and we don't really
necessarily pay attention

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:53.790
to the quality of the air.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:59.070
And so we recently released
27 years worth of EPA data

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:02.410
that shows all the different
particulates in the atmosphere,

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:06.930
and for thousands of
locations around the US.

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:10.710
And we're working on
some future stuff that

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:14.070
will show even more real-time
information about what's

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:16.170
going on around the world.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:18.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice, that
sounds really useful and scary.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:21.111
[LAUGHS] So we're kind
