00:24:21.111 --> 00:24:22.860
but is there anything
else that you'd like

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:24.060
to mention before we finish?

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:25.810
MIKE HAMBERG: Yeah,
so as we mentioned,

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.600
we've been partnering
with NOAA, and we

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.000
have lots of really great
partners in the data space.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:34.170
And quick success story
that I can share--

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:35.790
in the first few
months of this year,

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:39.750
we diffused about 200
times the amount of traffic

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.420
that they would have
expected on their datasets

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:44.140
with people using
the BigQuery instead.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:46.110
So this is real
savings for them,

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:48.530
because they don't have to
incur that cost of both load

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:51.520
on their datasets, and
also all those egress

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.490
charges that they get.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.360
This is saving taxpayer dollars,
and also making their data

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.170
more accessible and available.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:01.470
And that's a big, important
goal for the government.

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:02.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Awesome, so I guess

